<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
                           xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0"
                           xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>NAS Access by KerBridge</displayName>
  <description>Cloud sign-in for network drives.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>

      <string id="SUPPORTED_NAS_ACCESS_1_0">NAS Access by KerBridge 0.9.2 or later</string>

      <string id="NasAccess">NAS Access by KerBridge</string>
      <string id="NasAccess_Help">Settings for the NAS Access agent, which signs the user in to the cloud identity provider and puts a Kerberos ticket where Windows finds it.

Every setting here overrides the user's own choice in the agent's Settings window, and the control there shows the managed value instead of offering to change it. A setting left Not Configured leaves the choice with the user, or with the default the deployment publishes.</string>

      <string id="BrokerUrl">Broker address</string>
      <string id="BrokerUrl_Help">The HTTPS address of the KerBridge broker this machine authenticates against, for example https://kerbridge.example.site.

Enabled: the agent uses this address and the Settings field becomes read-only.

Disabled or Not Configured: the agent uses the address the user entered, or the one a _kerbridge._tcp SRV record in this machine's own DNS domain names.

The certificate is validated against the machine's trust store either way, so the LAN CA root has to be installed there first.</string>

      <string id="Autostart">Start the agent when the user signs in</string>
      <string id="Autostart_Help">Whether the agent starts with the user's session, and signs in again by itself when Windows can supply the credential silently.

Enabled: the agent registers itself to start at sign-in and the checkbox in its Settings window reads on and cannot be changed. This takes effect the first time the agent runs on an account: the entry is per-user, so nothing can write one for a user who has never run it. Install the MSI with AUTOSTART=1 where the very first sign-in has to start it.

Disabled: the agent removes its own start-at-sign-in entry. It does not remove a machine-wide entry that the installer wrote with AUTOSTART=1; only an administrator can do that, and the agent says so in its log.

Not Configured: the user's own choice stands.

The entry is per-user by necessity. The ticket has to land in the interactive user's own logon session, so the agent has to be started by that session.</string>

      <string id="WindowsSignIn">Use the Windows account to sign in</string>
      <string id="WindowsSignIn_Help">Whether the agent asks Windows' own token broker (WAM) for the token before it opens a browser.

Enabled: the agent tries Windows first. On an Entra-joined machine this makes sign-in silent. Anything that goes wrong falls through to the browser.

Disabled: every sign-in goes through the browser.

Not Configured: the user's own choice stands, and the agent tries Windows first by default.

This needs ms-appx-web://microsoft.aad.brokerplugin/&lt;client id&gt; registered as a redirect URI on the public client in the tenant.</string>

      <string id="NtlmFallbackRecovery">Offer to repair a stuck NTLM fallback</string>
      <string id="NtlmFallbackRecovery_Help">Whether the agent detects the SMB redirector's stuck NTLM fallback and offers the elevated LanmanWorkstation restart that clears it.

Enabled: the agent watches for the fallback and asks the user before it repairs.

Disabled: the whole mechanism is off. A stuck fallback is then cleared only by a reboot or by IT, and the agent does not name it.

Not Configured: the user's own choice stands, and the mechanism is on by default.

The fallback can never succeed on its own: the realm holds no password for a cloud identity.</string>

      <string id="GrantFor">Authorize this machine for a specific account</string>
      <string id="GrantFor_Help">The account a device grant on this machine works as - a login name, or a literal kb1| identity value. For an unattended machine that has to hold a ticket as a service account rather than as whoever last signed in.

Enabled: the agent authorizes itself for this account and shows the name rather than offering to change it.

Disabled or Not Configured: the machine authorizes itself for whoever signs in on it.

This is not an access control. The broker checks the delegate group whatever the machine asks for; this only decides what it asks for.</string>

    </stringTable>
    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="BrokerUrl">
        <textBox refId="BrokerUrl">
          <label>Broker address</label>
          <defaultValue>https://kerbridge.example.site</defaultValue>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="GrantFor">
        <textBox refId="GrantFor">
          <label>Account name</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
